      "id": "both-close-orders",
      "text": "On a fresh pipe, close the writer with a custom error W and let it return. A Read before the reader closes returns (0, W). Now close the reader with a custom error R. Later Reads and Writes both return (0, ErrClosedPipe). In the reverse order, a Write between the closes returns (0, R), and both operations again return (0, ErrClosedPipe) after the second close. The second close stores its own end's error even though the shared done channel is already closed. With plain writer Close, the same sequence makes a Read return EOF between closes and ErrClosedPipe after both.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 110,
        "fragment": "rerr == nil && werr != nil"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the documented guarantees about the peer receiving the supplied error or EOF apply only while the peer's own end remains open, or should the first close's result remain observable after both ends close?"
    },
    {
      "id": "close-before-next-transfer",
      "text": "With both ends open, start a five-byte Write and let a Read take and acknowledge two bytes. With no further reader available, close the reader with a non-nil error R. The blocked Write wakes and returns (2, R), and a later Write returns (0, R) while the writer end remains open. If the reader closes before any handoff and no Read can receive the data, the blocked Write returns (0, R). In the mirror case, a Read waiting with no writer wakes and returns (0, EOF) when the writer calls plain Close.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 92,
