    {
      "id": "close-around-handoff",
      "text": "With both ends open, let a Read receive all the bytes of a pending Write, then close the reader before the count is acknowledged. The Read still copies the bytes and sends the count. It returns the full count and nil, and the Write returns the full count and nil. If the close happens before the handoff but after both operations pass their initial checks, a ready data handoff can still win over a ready done channel. An operation that first checks done after closure returns a close error without transferring data.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 88,
        "fragment": "nw := <-p.rdCh"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should a data handoff remain possible after closure when the Read and Write already passed their initial checks, or should closure prevent every handoff that has yet to occur?"
    },
    {
      "id": "zero-length-operations",
      "text": "On a fresh open pipe, start Write with an empty slice and no reader. It waits until a Read receives the slice and acknowledges zero, then both calls return (0, nil). Conversely, when a Read with an empty buffer meets a pending nonempty Write, the Read returns (0, nil) and the writer retains every byte, waiting again until another Read consumes data or a close ends the Write.",
      "citation": {
